WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.078 --> 00:00:02.661 line:15%
(upbeat music)

2
00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.320
Hey everybody, welcome to Roll On,

3
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.570
where today we're gonna peer into the abyss

4
00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:25.510
in all its beauty and pain

5
00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:29.330 line:15%
and try to make some sense out of this extraordinary planet

6
00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:33.400 line:15%
and its curious and oftentimes confusing humans.

7
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.070
You guys know the deal,

8
00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.210
we rip from the headlines of our mad world,

9
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.380
we address some human stories

10
00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.500
from the front lines of endurance,

11
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.860
a few items that have captured our fancy

12
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.030
over the last couple weeks,

13
00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.300
we answer listener questions, and we do it all

14
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:53.740
with a light dusting of frivolity and randomness.

15
00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.660
But first, of course, we have to welcome

16
00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:59.600
the misplaced beat poet of the newest millennium,

17
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.355
Mr. Adam Skolnick.

18
00:01:01.355 --> 00:01:04.630
How you going, as the Aussies would say?

19
00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:09.590
Well, I don't know actually, to be honest.

20
00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.790
Oh, come on, let's get the energy up, right?

21
00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.330
It's been a couple of weeks.

22
00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.410
I know, but its been brought to my attention recently

23
00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:19.330
that I've been saying things without my own knowledge,

24
00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.560
saying things like, "What's up gang?"

25
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.267
And "Let's rock and roll crew."

26
00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:27.078
And I don't know Rich.
Where is that coming from?

27
00:01:27.078 --> 00:01:28.930
I don't know, it's hard for me to deal with.

28
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:29.933
I didn't even know I was saying it.

29
00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:31.363
It's an age thing?

30
00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:33.713
I think it's a dad thing.

31
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.240
I've so far been able to stave off the dad bod,

32
00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.680
but the dad brain is taking over.

33
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.490
Yeah, you can't stop that, it's a force of nature.

34
00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.700
Never in my wildest nightmares did I ever think

35
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.670
I'd ever say something like, "Let's rock and roll, crew."

36
00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.340
Well, the dad brain,

37
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.660
wait till the kid gets a little bit older

38
00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:01.420
because you're persistently reminded

39
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.240
about your dad energy and how uncool it is.

40
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.110
(Adam laughing)

41
00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:06.943
Can I be--

42
00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.510
That's what being a dad is about.

43
00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.000
Can I--
Just embrace it.

44
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:12.833
I will embrace it.

45
00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:15.480
Can I shrivel back into my dry sardonic self

46
00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.400
at some point, like age after age 14?

47
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.870
Just don't expect that it's gonna go

48
00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.410
over well with the kid.

49
00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:24.500
Nothing goes over well. (laughs)

50
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.810
Do you say things like, "What's up gang?"

51
00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:29.210
Oh, I do it on purpose now.

52
00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:32.810
I play it up, just ratchet, like just lean into it.

53
00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:33.643
It's more fun that way.

54
00:02:33.643 --> 00:02:35.538
And like, I'm running.

55
00:02:35.538 --> 00:02:38.400
I like how big of an eye roll can I get for this one?

56
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.330
Exactly, that's what I'm gonna have to do.

57
00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:44.800
Right now, he's just happy-go-lucky 18-month-old.

58
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.050
I was running this morning,

59
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.940
just this morning, and passed another dad,

60
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.743
who was walking with his two stroller dads,

61
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.290
the sun's not quite up yet.

62
00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:56.200
And I said, "Hey dad, how's it going?"

63
00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.370
Like, what's going on?

64
00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:58.403
What is happening?

65
00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:00.840
What is going on?

66
00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.870
That globe-trotting, serious journalist energy

67
00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:08.850
is being displaced with a suburban mindset.

68
00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:09.950
I used to be civilized.

69
00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:11.650
I used, or uncivilized.

70
00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.640
Now I'm more civilized, but it feels less civilized.

71
00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.010
I don't know how to say it.

72
00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:17.450
Like I said, just lean into it.

73
00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:18.283
I'm leaning in.

74
00:03:18.283 --> 00:03:22.850
But the dad bod issue, we have news from the front lines.

75
00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:26.130
Right, so four by four by 48 happened.

76
00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:26.963
Yeah.

77
00:03:26.963 --> 00:03:27.796
Yes.

78
00:03:27.796 --> 00:03:30.670
The Goggins, the Annual Goggins Challenge in the books.

79
00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:34.400
The Annual Goggins Ultra Festival for the uninitiated.

80
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.800
Yeah, how'd it go for you?

81
00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:39.330
I did, I had injuries going in,

82
00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:42.560
so I wasn't really prepared this time.

83
00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.245
And Jason came up to me, I think last time I was here,

84
00:03:46.245 --> 00:03:48.207
and said, "Are you gonna do the Goggins Challenge?"

85
00:03:48.207 --> 00:03:50.270
And I hadn't even thought about doing it

86
00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.460
because I'd hurt my back at the beginning of January.

87
00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.490
But as his co-author, you have to.

88
00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:55.610
I did--
No choice.

89
00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:56.443
Right.

90
00:03:56.443 --> 00:04:00.270
So what happened was like my back injury ended,

91
00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:03.740
so I ran probably 12 miles in January.

92
00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.130
And then in February, like I could start running again,

93
00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:07.620
maybe the second week in February.

94
00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.740
I probably ran less than 60 miles in the two months.

95
00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:11.890
Definitely, less than 60 miles

96
00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:15.420
in the two months coming into March.

97
00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:16.570
And so I was thinking, all right,

98
00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:17.760
let me just see how the body is.

99
00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.310
So on the Sunday and Tuesday, I did some repetitions.

100
00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.140
I did three, three-mile runs on Sunday.

101
00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.200
I did four twos on Tuesday.

102
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.780
And if my body held up, I was gonna give it a shot,

103
00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.410
but I hadn't felt good running even after even coming back.

104
00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.610
I had not had a good run since the, before I got hurt,

105
00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:35.973
I hadn't felt good, my everything was off.

106
00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:41.530
And for some reason, I was ended up faster by almost

107
00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:45.550
an hour in cumulative time, I felt good, my body felt good.

108
00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:48.440
And it's a grind, it's a grind doing it.

109
00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:49.550
April did it with me as well.

110
00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.930
She took 72 hours, because she couldn't do

111
00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:52.980
the middle of the night stuff

112
00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.820
because we, no one's there to watch Zuma and he wakes up.

113
00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:00.230
So she had to carry it a third day.

114
00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:01.830
She didn't have to do the middle of the night,

115
00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.430
she had to carry it a third day.

116
00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.450
And Saturday, the winds were howling,

117
00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:07.590
I mean, the winds were bad here,

118
00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.600
but the winds were like 40 mile,

119
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.643
like over 30 miles an hour in the desert.

120
00:05:11.643 --> 00:05:12.673
You were out in the desert.

121
00:05:12.673 --> 00:05:15.323
Yeah, and it was like crazy winds, all of Saturday.

122
00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:17.800
Mild hallucinations in the middle

123
00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:19.360
of the night, you see like a street sign,

124
00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:20.193
you think it's a coyote coming

125
00:05:20.193 --> 00:05:23.273
to get you, like just crazy stuff on the second night.

126
00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:28.620
But it's running at night, it's the most nightlife I've had

127
00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.610
since Zuma's been, actually since last year,

128
00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:32.060
since this time, last year.

129
00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:34.530
It was the most nightlife I've had in a year.

130
00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:35.697
Well, congrats.
Thank you.

131
00:05:35.697 --> 00:05:38.150
And congrats to Jason as well.

132
00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:39.190
Yes, congrats.

133
00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:42.110
Two team members banked it this year.

134
00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:46.129
And it's interesting that you had a what sounds like

135
00:05:46.129 --> 00:05:48.740
a more pleasant experience doing it this year.

136
00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.210
And I think that has to have something

137
00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:52.210
to do with the fact that you've done it before.

138
00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:54.890
So you have that history, you know can do it,

139
00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:56.820
so it's not as anxiety provoking,

140
00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:59.830
even if you hadn't trained adequately for it.

141
00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:01.790
Like you can kind of wrap your head around like,

142
00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:03.490
okay, I know how this is gonna go.

143
00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:05.920
Yeah, the wild card was

144
00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.430
will my glutes get twingy, will a lower back lockup?

145
00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.670
And so, that was the only...

146
00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:14.870
Like, the four mile runs are one thing,

147
00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:16.460
but that was the wild card

148
00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.950
that you couldn't really plan for.

149
00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:20.420
But in the end, like I said, and since then,

150
00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.570
it's almost like this jump-started training camp,

151
00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:24.260
'cause since then my running has been better.

152
00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:27.810
I felt better, I feel stronger, so I don't know.

153
00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:29.480
Last time it was like, I did it.

154
00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:31.150
And then I took a little bit

155
00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:32.690
of a break, and got back in the ocean.

156
00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:36.350
And this time I'm kind of riding the momentum a little bit

157
00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:37.650
and trying to get that zone two

158
00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:39.710
back to where I want it to be.

159
00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:44.660
Meanwhile, the mythos of David Goggins continues to grow.

160
00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:45.630
Incredible.
I mean, he was amazing

161
00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:46.463
those videos.
Incredible.

162
00:06:46.463 --> 00:06:47.680
That he shared, like

163
00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.270
so many people, he was going up and down the coast

164
00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:51.800
and popping up here and there.

165
00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:53.990
Started in Sacramento area.

166
00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:55.960
And like helicopters following him

167
00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:58.660
and thousands of people, and him leaning out

168
00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:01.230
of SUVs, screaming, and people going nuts.

169
00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:03.070
Like it was really something.

170
00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:05.670
Yeah, he, no, he's a full blown star, you know?

171
00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.900
And he's got like the sheriff helicopters shouting out

172
00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:12.547
tributes to him and he flew to Ventura.

173
00:07:12.547 --> 00:07:14.450
He did the middle of the night once, he kept

174
00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:19.450
with just, he and Jennifer, and then had those meetups.

175
00:07:19.453 --> 00:07:23.110
Hermosa, I think was the largest from what they told me.

176
00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:27.953
Hermosa, the over a thousand people down the strand there.

177
00:07:27.953 --> 00:07:30.850
I mean, just what an experience to be there.

178
00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:32.650
And he signed every single book.

179
00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:36.380
He took a picture with every single person that turned up.

180
00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:37.830
He did it all the way through,

181
00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:40.330
and then they had to immediately race down to Costa Mesa.

182
00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:44.160
So like, and get there just in time, and do it all again.

183
00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:47.780
And apparently the final run he finished in sub sevens.

184
00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:49.017
Right, of course.

185
00:07:49.017 --> 00:07:51.040
(Adam laughing)

186
00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:52.763
Good for him, man, unbelievable.

187
00:07:54.319 --> 00:07:56.210
You've been getting out with the whales?

188
00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:58.970
Yes, so it's whale migration season here

189
00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:00.820
in Malibu, so I'm sure everyone...

190
00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:05.290
Well you know about, and people here, local,

191
00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:06.620
in Malibu know about.

192
00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:09.980
So the gray whales are migrating back up past,

193
00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:11.890
Monterey first, and then Alaska

194
00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:14.403
and onto Points North, Bering Sea, I believe.

195
00:08:16.304 --> 00:08:18.610
And so yeah, you get lucky sometimes you get lucky.

196
00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:20.480
So the Malibu artists kind of came.

197
00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.700
Usually I meet him in Santa Monica, in the Santa Monica Bay,

198
00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.760
but he, this time we met up

199
00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:29.450
at Dume and he flew and was able to get me, I got,

200
00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:32.350
I was swimming and barely saw, I turned and took a breath.

201
00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:35.000
And all of a sudden I see a whale fluke

202
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.950
about 50 feet outside me.

203
00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:39.400
And he happened to get it from the whales perspective.

204
00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.180
Yeah, I saw that.
So that was pretty cool.

205
00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:42.910
So I'm hoping that we have more meetups,

206
00:08:42.910 --> 00:08:45.130
because when you have a spotter, it's easier.

207
00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:46.480
I actually didn't see him

208
00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:48.550
in the air and hopefully we can start

209
00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:50.850
to coordinate because then you can actually

210
00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:53.320
maybe look under and see something, but--

211
00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:55.610
You need some kind of waterproof earpiece

212
00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:57.682
where he can direct you

213
00:08:57.682 --> 00:08:59.300
where to swim, get right on top of the whale.

214
00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:00.590
Well, you're not supposed to do that, right.

215
00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:02.870
So you're not supposed to, if you see a whale out there

216
00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:05.410
or even dolphins, you're not supposed to get in their way,

217
00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:07.070
you're not supposed to intentionally swim there.

218
00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:08.930
So what we do is we swim there anyway.

219
00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:12.110
As anyone can tell, we swim all year round there.

220
00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:13.260
And so when they come around,

221
00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:15.470
they have to come to you or you have to just get lucky

222
00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:17.530
and stumble upon them and then it's fine.

223
00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:18.870
And we're not doing anything.

224
00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:20.390
We never touch them, we never do anything.

225
00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:22.580
We don't even get that close, why would you?

226
00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:25.470
And so I feel like it's pretty good

227
00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:27.740
and we're not, we're never in their way.

228
00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:30.110
So it's nice when it's more accidental,

229
00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:33.300
but at the same time, when you're a floating head,

230
00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:34.990
you can't even see where you are.

231
00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:36.909
So if it's, it could be 20 feet away

232
00:09:36.909 --> 00:09:40.150
from you and you miss it, especially if there's some motion

233
00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:40.983
on the surface.

234
00:09:40.983 --> 00:09:44.590
So it's nice to be able to have a friend like

235
00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:47.420
the Malibu artist who does some epic videos.

236
00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:49.170
That's pretty cool.

237
00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:52.770
Let's do a quick obit for your man, Jack Kerouac,

238
00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.100
who probably never lapsed

239
00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:56.206
into any kind of dadism at any point.

240
00:09:56.206 --> 00:09:57.039
No.

241
00:09:57.039 --> 00:09:57.872
In his life.

242
00:09:57.872 --> 00:09:59.790
His niece is a listener of yours.

243
00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:00.623
Oh really?

244
00:10:00.623 --> 00:10:02.310
His great niece is a listener of yours.

245
00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:05.560
And she followed me and then we had a little back and forth.

246
00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:08.350
She's a photographer and we had a back and forth.

247
00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:10.270
I said, "Kerouac, interesting."

248
00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:11.820
And it's her great uncle.

249
00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:12.653
Amazing.

250
00:10:12.653 --> 00:10:15.150
Yeah and I said, this is the greatest, this is,

251
00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:18.830
I was so touched and yeah, so she's a listener.

252
00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:22.593
And he just turned 100, he died at 47.

253
00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:26.420
But to me he's obviously,

254
00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:28.860
that's where we got the holy inside milk from.

255
00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:30.950
That's kind of where I cribbed it from, one

