And I have to say that it has led me to turn back

513
00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:50.940
to the wisdom of past podcast guest, like Susan David,

514
00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:53.330
who writes and talks about emotional agility

515
00:21:53.330 --> 00:21:56.740
and Jessica Lahey, in her book, "The Gift of Failure,"

516
00:21:56.740 --> 00:21:58.710
like this idea that

517
00:21:58.710 --> 00:22:01.730
these experiences cultivate emotional agility.

518
00:22:01.730 --> 00:22:04.030
But this is a kid who really needed a win.

519
00:22:04.030 --> 00:22:05.540
The pandemic has been very rough

520
00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:07.989
on him and this was a North Star

521
00:22:07.989 --> 00:22:10.780
and it got like pulled out from underneath him.

522
00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:11.741
After doing great work.

523
00:22:11.741 --> 00:22:13.310
At that age it's traumatic, right?

524
00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:18.100
And to see your kid in the midst of a trauma is just,

525
00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:19.253
it's very difficult.

526
00:22:20.298 --> 00:22:21.298
I'm sorry.

527
00:22:21.298 --> 00:22:22.131
So that's kind of going on in the background.

528
00:22:22.131 --> 00:22:23.460
So if I start to space out

